  "correctness_argument": "Two changes, each so that a released value is stored as released (owner rule 2026-10-12, 'the data is the data').\n\n(1) EnvironmentResponsePhenotype gains environment_response_z_score (#879), an optional field in its own ADDITIVE block with a validator that requires a finite number and a numeric response. Wang 2018 releases a 'Z score' column beside the fitness on every guide row (Supplementary Data 6-10) and every gene row (Supplementary Data 5); equation (4) defines it as the fitness divided by the standard deviation of a normal fitted to the 400 non-targeting guides. No existing field carries it: it is not an uncertainty (the sigma is the null distribution's scale, so it must never feed environment_response_uncertainty or the derived SE), not a p-value and not the moderated t of RB-TnSeq. Every record that does not set it serializes with None, so no accepted record is refused.\n\n(2) HeterologousPathwayPerturbation.source_organism becomes value-or-ProvenanceGap (#915): the leaf now lists HashableProvenanceGapMixin first and re-declares source_organism as `str | None`, REQUIRED with no default, with a validator (_require_value_or_gap, the Media.name pattern of #901-#903) that refuses a None that carries no gap. Three loaders (Menasalvas 2025, Brunk 2016, Foo 2014) had to write the sentinel string 'unreported' into a field that reads as an organism name, because the field forced a str. A typed gap is the policy's route for a value the source does not state (Rule 3, Rule 5: the field stays required, nothing is weakened). The schema-impact tool reports the re-declaration as 'added required field', which is why contract_tightened is claimed beside typed_gap: the accepted set is every stated organism, as before, plus None beside a gap naming the field. The widening is on this leaf only, as PromoterReplacementPerturbation re-declares crispr, so GeneAdditionPerturbation and the yeast gene-addition closures do not move. The mixin is listed first so the leaf still hashes (Genotype compares perturbations as a set), which a test pins.",
  "measurement_type_decision": false,
  "source_quotes": [],
  "alternatives_rejected": [
    {
      "alternative": "store the Wang 2018 Z score nowhere because it is fitness / sigma with sigma constant per screen",
      "why_rejected": "it is a released column; the owner's rule stores what the source releases, and dropping it leaves the gene-level hit call (Z, FPR, FDR) incomplete on the record"
    },
    {
      "alternative": "carry FPRvalue in a new field",
      "why_rejected": "the paper states it is a p-value: 'We then took the classical Storey-Tibshirani approach to convert the FPR values (a particular type of P value) to Q values', so environment_response_p_value (#863) carries it and the Q value is the adjusted p-value with its method named"
    },
    {
      "alternative": "make GeneAdditionPerturbation.source_organism optional at the base class",
      "why_rejected": "moves every yeast gene-addition closure for a gap only bacterial production loaders need; the leaf-level re-declaration has precedent (PromoterReplacementPerturbation.crispr)"
    },
    {
      "alternative": "infer the five pIY670 organisms from Menasalvas Sheet 4's UniProt entry names",
      "why_rejected": "the sheet never states which part token each enzyme is, so a mapping would be an inference stored as a fact; the owner asked that tokens be mapped only where Sheet 4 states them, and it states none"
    }
  ],
